Z||000009||Cliff Pennock||08-19-2000||03:33 AM||||Well, nothing much has changed over the past two years...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I get a lot of requests for information on the Digital Dash from the States and I usually give everyone the same answer: that I'm not selling it in the States... yet.<P>The reason is because I want to sell the dashboards through a dealer and I simply haven't found one yet.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||08-19-2000||09:22 AM||||What does it take to become a dealer?  I'll sell 'em for ya!  Of course, I'd have to have one in my car to use as a demo.<P>Seriously, what is the advantage to having someone in the States sell them?  Bulk shipping??  Time constraints on your part??  (I know you're busier than heck.)  Although you seem to have the lowest price for the best dash, there couldn't be a lot of markup if you went through a middleman (or woman).  A grand isn't bad (although I don't have it), but by the time a dealer paid for shipping to the States, advertising, a slight markup, then shipping to the customer, it seems like the dash would end up costing someone more like $1500 or so.  Tell me where I'm in error, please.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Cliff Pennock||08-19-2000||02:38 PM||||Well, you said it yourself - I'm just too busy to handle everything myself. Be aware that although the FDD-X1 is very reliable, the slightest thing wrong in the car itself can cause it to not function properly anymore. I can give a couple of examples of this. After installation in a Fiero once, it turned out the RPM gauge was going nuts. Every time you would throttle a little bit it would give all kinds of readings except the proper one. It took me about a month to find out what was wrong: this Fiero didn't have the proper tachfilter... The original tachfilter is no longer being made and GM's official replacement tachfilter isn't working as it is supposed to be. That's why I ended up making my own tachfilters (which are exactly the same as the original). Another example: I had a Dash in a car that sometimes had a jumping tach and speedometer. That also took me a while to find the cause: it was a bad connector on the ignition coil. Last example: my own dashboard would reset itself every other minute or so since the last 4 weeks. As you may know my car died on me last weekend and it turned out to be the ignition coil. I changed the coil and everything was fine again - including the dashboard! It turns out the FDD-X1 will warn you for  problems with your car long before the ECM can.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The FDD-X1 is incredibly accurate but the downside to this accuracy is that it's also sensitive to electronic disturbances in the car. Any Fiero is at least 12 years old so chances are there is a sensor or other electronic component that is causing some spikes in the electronic system. It took me 6 months to design the dashboard and 18 months to make it as insensitive to these spikes as possible.<P>To keep the costs of the dash low, I'm thinking of having them manufactured in the States. WHat I mean is, I would give all design schematics and building instructions to the dealer/distributor except for the brain of the dash: the programmed processor. This means I only have to ship the processors. This also minimizes the chance that anything gets damaged during shipping.<P>The FDD-X1 is incredibly reliable and accurate (thanks to its design) as long as the car itself is in good shape. So far I only have had one dashboard returned because it was having problems - turned out I forgot to solder one small 10ct component... Duh!<P>I enjoy my own dashboard very much. Since the Fiero is so low, every car that passes me has a good view on it. It happens <B>very</B> often that I get passed by a car which then slows down and starts driving beside me so they can have a good look!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||rockcrawl||08-19-2000||04:33 PM||||Cliff, Those are the same types of problems I had with my Cyberdyne guages. They were very sensitive. I had to run shielded cables to all the sensors throughout the engine compartment (V8) to prevent interference. I guess thats just the nature of the digital guages.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000013||Cliff Pennock||08-19-2000||04:58 PM||||Yes, the Fiero is an electronics engineer's nightmare! Most of it because it's a polyester car so spikes have nowhere to go. A shielded cable sometimes helps but most of the interference is actually entering the electronics through ground. The easiest way of protecting the dash is by putting it in a metal box like the ECM. But that makes it <B>very</B> hard to read the displays...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||Monkeyman||08-20-2000||07:03 AM||||So, what does it take to build the dash?  Is is simply a matter of gathering off-the-shelf parts and soldering them together?  Or would someone have to fabricate anything?  If it's just a matter of soldering some readily available parts together, it would be easy (although very time consuming).  If it required some fabrication...  How long does it take you to build one dash?  Granted, as the designer, you could probably build one in your sleep (ever tried that?? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">).  What would it cost someone to do this?  Just wondering since I'm about to have my hours cut in half (a <B>very</B> good thing! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) and need some justification for the $3000 I just spent to have a shed built, power run to it, and to have it finished inside (insulated, heat, A/C, etc).||||reg||1
